#
# TARGETS (repo-root relative; apply with -p1 from the repo root):
#   daemon/internal/ingest/alpaca/client.go          (modified)
#   daemon/internal/ingest/alpaca/client_test.go     (modified — test helper only)
#   daemon/internal/ingest/alpaca/client429_test.go  (NEW FILE)
#
# HOW TO APPLY — use core.autocrlf=false, it matters here:
#   cd "C:/Users/Nicholas_N/Desktop/claude code/signaldeck"
#   git -c core.autocrlf=false apply --check drafts/patches/alpaca-429.patch
#   git -c core.autocrlf=false apply         drafts/patches/alpaca-429.patch
#   cd daemon && go test ./internal/ingest/alpaca/ -count=1
#
#   Why the flag: this box has core.autocrlf=true globally and .gitattributes
#   has no *.go rule, so a plain `git apply` REWRITES client.go from LF to CRLF
#   and every one of its 441 lines shows up as changed. With the flag the apply
#   is byte-faithful (verified: client.go stays LF, client_test.go stays CRLF).
#   Note client_test.go really is CRLF in the working tree while client.go is
#   LF — the hunks below deliberately carry each file's own line endings, so do
#   not normalise this patch file.
#
# WHAT IT DOES
#   fetchBarsPage (client.go:196-204 pre-patch) had no 429 handling: it turned
#   HTTP 429 straight into an error, and backfill's caller re-drove the symbol
#   unpaced — the observed live retry storm.
#
#   The multi-symbol sibling fetchMultiBarsPage ALREADY had a 429 back-off loop
#   (client.go:404-428 pre-patch: bounded retries, doubling wait, ctx-aware
#   sleep). Rather than write a second idiom, this patch EXTRACTS that loop into
#   one shared helper — getRetrying — and routes BOTH bars paths through it.
#   The multi path is a net DELETION (~35 lines out, ~15 in) and inherits the
#   three things it was missing. Both callers of the fix route through one
#   function, so there is no sibling path left unfixed.
#
#   On top of the extracted loop, getRetrying adds:
#     * Retry-After honoured in both RFC 9110 forms (delay-seconds AND
#       HTTP-date), capped at maxRetryAfter (60s) so an absurd or hostile header
#       cannot wedge a worker. Absent/negative/past/unparseable values fall back
#       to the ladder instead of erroring.
#     * Full jitter over [d/2, d] on the exponential ladder, so several workers
#       throttled on the same tick do not retry on the same tick.
#     * Bounded retries — bound UNCHANGED (maxBackfillRetries=4 => 5 requests).
#     * ctx-aware sleep on EVERY back-off (select on ctx.Done()).
#     * slog.Warn per throttle + slog.Error on give-up, and the exhaustion error
#       is RETURNED. Throttling is never silent; the final failure is never
#       swallowed. slog is the daemon's existing convention (17 files) and its
#       default handler is installed at cmd/signaldeckd/main.go:51, so these
#       land in the daemon log.
#
#   client_test.go gets a 3-line change to the existing fastPages helper so it
#   shrinks backoff429 as well as pagePause. Without it the PRE-EXISTING
#   TestBackfillHTTPError (serves a permanent 429) would newly sleep the real
#   ~30s ladder. Measured after the patch: 0.15s.
#
# NOT INCLUDED (deliberate)
#   * ValidateSymbol still has no 429 retry — one-shot call to the paper-API
#     host, not on the storm path. Route it through getRetrying if it ever shows
#     up in the logs.
#   * No dq_event row. The alpaca Client holds no *store.Store (the store is a
#     parameter of the backfill entrypoints, not a client field), so emitting
#     dq_events from the fetch layer means threading a store through two call
#     chains. The brief allowed "a dq_event OR a structured log"; slog is the
#     smaller change. Upgrade path if throttling must be queryable: have
#     backfill/backfillMulti detect the returned "rate limited" error and call
#     InsertDQ(md.DQEvent{Kind:"alpaca_rate_limited"}) there, where the store is
#     already in hand.
#
# VERIFICATION — all run in a temp scratch copy of the module, never in daemon/.
# NO LIVE ALPACA CALL IS MADE; every test is served by httptest.
#   git -c core.autocrlf=false apply -p1        -> exit 0 (onto pristine files)
#   gofmt -l (client.go, client429_test.go)     -> clean
#     (client_test.go is gofmt-flagged BEFORE and AFTER — it is CRLF; ignoring
#      line endings the patched file matches gofmt output exactly)
#   go vet ./internal/ingest/alpaca/            -> exit 0
#   go build ./...                              -> exit 0
#   go test ./internal/ingest/alpaca/ -count=1  -> ok ... 8.368s
#   The two PRE-EXISTING multi-symbol 429 tests still pass unchanged.
